{"availability":"available","full":"luizfnsilva/closure_drift","name":"closure_drift","description":"Does your version label name exactly one version of your code? A read-only, zero-dependency detector for version-label drift in git repositories.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-05T11:17:37Z","updated":"2026-09-15T13:29:13Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21763931","topics":["cli","content-addressing","git","python","reproducibility","reproducible-builds","research-software","software-provenance","supply-chain","versioning"],"demo":null,"color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.983Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Does your version label name exactly one version of your code?","lodash publishes 78 labels across 400 tags; 69 of them cover more than one code state. polars: 51 labels, 40 in drift, one of them covering 16. Neither project is badly run — both are in drift by a property of their release scheme, and neither could have noticed from what they recorded.","That is the failure this tool measures. When you publish something and address it by (input, version), that address is sound only if the version identifies exactly one state of the producing code. Nothing enforces it — the label is a string a human edits. When two code states share a label, one address denotes two outputs, and the system cannot notice, because the label is the only thing it recorded."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/luizfnsilva/closure_drift/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"72371cdbf27c1b2d6bc6550367c7f46d3ccf37770840d6313161b59394fce3ae","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T21:17:10.98